| RunReadtest3 - WHICH bytes go wrong when read() fills stack memory the program has never touched, and when.  Every case is its own program run (fresh stack).
| Output: ResultsReadtest3 in this directory (long).  Nothing is installed or changed: it copies one library file to this folder (case E) and deletes the copy.
| (the first EABI program run changes <Obey$Dir>, so remember our directory first)
Set RoTest$Dir <Obey$Dir>
Dir <RoTest$Dir>
Spool <RoTest$Dir>.ResultsReadtest3
Echo ===================== a simulation of the damage readtest2 showed, to compare with (nothing is read from the file system)
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -p 408 model 32
Echo ===================== A. the plain case, and a control that copies with UnixLib's memcpy instead of read()
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 memcpy 64
Echo ===================== A2. the same copy by the CPU in user mode, ONE kind of store instruction at a time (are page faults restarted properly?)
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 cpu-strb 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 cpu-str 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 cpu-strd 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 cpu-ldm 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 cpu-vstr 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 cpu-vstm 64
Echo ===================== A3. a module (CryptRand) fills the buffer in supervisor mode: /dev/urandom, no file system involved
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 urandom 64
Echo ===================== B. memory pages that are mapped already: the upper half written first, only the top page written first
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 upper 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 top 64
Echo ===================== C. the same read in smaller pieces
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -c 65536 read 128
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -c 4096 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -c 512 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -c 100 read 8
Echo ===================== D. the buffer shifted against the memory pages (-p) and the file read from another offset (-s)
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -p 1000 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -p 2048 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -s 100000 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -s 100000 -p 2048 read 64
Echo ===================== E. another file system: a copy of the library in this folder (LanMan98) instead of the NVMe
Copy SharedLibs:lib.armeabihf.libgcc_s/so/1 <RoTest$Dir>.gccs_copy ~C~VF
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 -f <RoTest$Dir>.gccs_copy read 64
Delete <RoTest$Dir>.gccs_copy
Echo ===================== F. bigger (readtest2 had a clean memory page every 31 pages)
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest3 read 256
Spool
Echo Finished. Output is in <RoTest$Dir>.ResultsReadtest3
